  "image_name": "1b4ee568dfc6a5caad047ad9df387625.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how enveloped viruses exploit the ESCRT membrane-remodeling machinery to bud through cellular membranes, and compares several related topologies. The central theme is membrane scission away from the cytoplasm, with ESCRT-III and VPS4 acting at the narrow neck of a budding membrane.\n\nOne schematic shows herpesvirus nuclear egress at the nuclear envelope. A viral capsid buds into the perinuclear space after membrane deformation by the viral NEC complex, then recruits ALIX, ESCRT-III, and VPS4 to pinch off the budded particle. The resulting enveloped capsid is released into the space between the inner and outer nuclear membranes.\n\nA second example shows a membrane invagination that forms a replication compartment, labeled as a spherule. Here ESCRT factors, including TSG101, VPS4, and ESCRT-III, are concentrated at the constricted neck, which appears to maintain a protected channel while viral RNA replication occurs inside the vesicle-like structure.\n\nThe bottom comparison depicts outward budding at the plasma membrane, such as retroviral assembly driven by a viral protein like Gag. Again, ALIX/ESCRT factors assemble at the bud neck and VPS4 completes membrane fission, releasing an enveloped virion. Overall, the figure highlights a shared ESCRT-dependent scission mechanism used in distinct viral membrane-remodeling processes.",
